LLM architecture — how a question becomes an answer
1. User prompt
“Explain a failed payment.”

2. Tokenizer
Text → tokens → token IDs

3. Embedding layer
Token IDs → numerical vectors

4. Transformer layers
Attention + feed-forward networks

5. Output scores and decoding
Calculate token scores and select the next token

6. Generated response
Tokens are generated successively and decoded into text.

Component	Responsibility
Tokenizer	 - Splits text into units the model can process
Token IDs -	Represent tokens as vocabulary indices
Embedding layer	- Converts IDs into learned numerical vectors
Attention - 	Helps each token's representation incorporate relevant context
Feed-forward network	- Further transforms representations within each transformer layer
Output layer -	Produces scores for possible next tokens
Decoding -	Uses a selection strategy to choose the next token

The exact implementation varies between models. For example, a decoder-only Transformer is common in text-generating LLMs.
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Self-attention — use the context
It helps the model determine which other tokens are relevant when processing a token. For example, it helps connect “it” to the thing being discussed earlier in a sentence.

Feed-forward network — transform the information
It applies learned mathematical transformations to the token representations, helping the model build more useful features.

Self-Attention: Finds relationships between words to understand the context.
Feed-Forward Network (FFN): Processes the information to learn useful patterns.

Remember: Self-Attention = Connect information. Feed-Forward = Process information.
